      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2017 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": "A 78-year-old Caucasian man with significant vascular disease reported sudden onset of worsened vision during a routine wound-care appointment for nonhealing necrotic leg ulcers. He described painless blurring of vision with grey scotomas in his right eye, his only well-seeing eye, after trauma to the left eye as a child. He presented with retinal ischemia, a cotton wool spot, and optic nerve swelling. Temporal artery biopsy showed myxoid degeneration and extensive calcium deposition, which also was present on imaging throughout his carotid and vertebral arterial system-the clinical picture of calciphylaxis."
      },
